                  "id": "d8e35114-ec87-6bbc-9cbb-78abe79adce4",
                  "title": "Transparent Methods & Scope Limitations",
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "text": "Honest Limitation & Future Work Framing",
                      "type": "heading",
                      "level": 2
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Every study faces real constraints: convenience sampling, short follow-up windows, self-report bias, or single-region data. Stating limitations transparently alongside concrete ideas for how future research resolves them protects interpretive integrity without invalidating practical value.",
                      "type": "paragraph"
                    },
                    {
                      "term": "Interpretive Scope Bounds",
                      "text": "Clarifying explicitly to which contexts, timeframes, or populations study findings generalize cleanly—and where extrapolation remains premature.",
                      "type": "definition"
                    },
                    {
                      "body": "\"Because participants were volunteers across three mid-sized hospitals over 12 weeks, findings primarily inform short-term onboarding rather than multi-year retention.\"",
                      "type": "example",
                      "title": "Constructive Limitation Example"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "steps",
                      "items": [
                        "State exact sampling, instrument, or longitudinal boundaries neutrally.",
                        "Explain how those bounds affect interpretation direction without dramatic self-deprecation.",
                        "Pair key limits directly with sensible next-study designs."
                      ],
                      "title": "Applying Transparent Methods & Scope Limitations"
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Claiming \"global college behavior\" based solely on 34 psychology undergraduates at one campus drew immediate reviewer censure.",
                      "type": "case_study",
                      "title": "Hidden Convenience Bias",
                      "takeaway": "Bounding claims clearly to \"introductory cohort behaviors\" preserved valid exploratory insights intact."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Treat limitations matter-of-factly as standard scientific transparency—never sound ashamed of finite resources.",
                      "type": "callout",
                      "title": "Own Constraints Without Apologizing Excessively",
                      "variant": "tip"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "knowledge_check",
                      "prompt": "How should a research paper treat unavoidable methodological constraints?",
                      "options": [
                        "Omit them silently hoping reviewers skim",
                        "State scope bounds neutrally and explain carefully what future follow-up research should verify",
                        "Cancel submission completely"
                      ],
                      "explanation": "Honest scope framing models scientific maturity and guides subsequent study safely.",
                      "correctIndex": 1
