                    {
                      "text": "1:N Foreign Keys vs M:N Junction Tables",
                      "type": "heading",
                      "level": 2
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Represent one-to-many ownership with a direct foreign key (`courses.skill_id -> skills.id`), and represent many-to-many associations with a dedicated junction table (`course_skills(course_id, skill_id)` with composite primary key). Explicitly naming or tracking foreign-key constraints keeps queries unambiguous when both direct and junction paths exist.",
                      "type": "paragraph"
                    },
                    {
                      "term": "Junction Table",
                      "text": "An associative table holding two foreign keys as a composite primary key to model true many-to-many relationships cleanly.",
                      "type": "definition"
                    },
                    {
                      "body": "Primary competency lives on `courses.skill_id` (`courses_skill_id_fkey`), while multi-skill alignment lives in `course_skills` (`course_skills_skill_id_fkey`).",
                      "type": "example",
                      "title": "Dual Course-Skill Relationship"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "steps",
                      "items": [
                        "Identify entities and cardinality (1:1, 1:N, M:N) before writing SQL.",
                        "Use NOT NULL, UNIQUE, and CHECK constraints for domain invariants.",
                        "Choose intentional ON DELETE CASCADE vs SET NULL rules."
                      ],
                      "title": "Applying Normalization, Foreign Keys & Junction Tables"
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Adding proper foreign keys and unique(user_id, course_id) eliminated duplicate orphan enrollments permanently.",
                      "type": "case_study",
                      "title": "Orphaned Enrollment Records",
                      "takeaway": "Database constraints guard invariants even if two concurrent requests race."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Whenever two tables share both a direct FK and a junction table, qualify embeds/joins explicitly so ORM/PostgREST never guesses.",
                      "type": "callout",
                      "title": "Explicit Disambiguation",
                      "variant": "tip"
