                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Alexander Osterwalder’s 9-block Business Model Canvas groups neatly into **Front-Stage Value** (`Customer Segments`, `Value Propositions`, `Channels`, `Relationships`), **Back-Stage Engine** (`Key Activities`, `Resources`, `Partners`), and **Financial Equation** (`Revenue Streams` minus `Cost Structure`). When you shift from cheap self-serve software toward complex enterprise contracts, EVERY block shifts simultaneously (`high-touch concierge reps`, `longer legal cycles`, `annual upfront invoices`).",
                      "type": "paragraph"
                    },
                    {
                      "term": "Model Coherence",
                      "text": "Tight alignment across customer tier, sales channel cost, delivery complexity, and price point so acquisition/fulfillment spending never exceeds realistic customer lifetime revenue.",
                      "type": "definition"
                    },
                    {
                      "body": "Mismatch: $15/mo consumer app pitched via costly 45-minute human phone demos (CAC $250 vs LTV $60 -> bankrupt!). Coherent Match: $15/mo app acquired via viral self-serve sharing OR $250 human demo closing $3,000 annual enterprise licenses!",
                      "type": "example",
                      "title": "Checking Model Alignment"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "steps",
                      "items": [
                        "Color-code distinct Canvas cards whenever serving two separate buyer sides (e.g., Riders vs. Drivers!).",
                        "Trace arrows physically from each Value Proposition through exact Channel costs straight down to Pricing.",
                        "Test unit arithmetic immediately whenever swapping high-touch sales for low-price tiers."
                      ],
                      "title": "Applying The Business Model Canvas as an Interlocking Machine"
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "A boutique marketplace priced cheap $12 bookings while providing live concierge phone agents for every transaction—losing $18 net per booking!",
                      "type": "case_study",
                      "title": "Misaligned Canvas Collapse",
                      "takeaway": "Matching service intensity cleanly to margin capability heals broken business engines rapidly."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Always map separate buyer/user personas in distinct colors on marketplaces or ad-funded platforms—never scramble advertiser and consumer needs together!",
