            {
              "id": "5263deab-c38e-0929-4101-322633d34ccf",
              "title": "Module 2: Interests Over Positions & Multi-Variable Trades",
              "lessons": [
                {
                  "id": "bf64692a-cdb8-52db-4bd8-e7453010003e",
                  "title": "Positions vs. Underlying Interests (The Orange Story)",
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "text": "Uncovering Hidden Constraints & Shared Interests",
                      "type": "heading",
                      "level": 2
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Two siblings fought furiously over ONE last orange—splitting it 50/50 left both half-angry until they realized one needed strictly **rind zest for baking** and the other wanted **inner juice**! Always probe gently underneath rigid **Positions** (`\"Cut price 20%!\"`) to surface true **Interests** (`\"Why is cash upfront tight this Q2 specifically? Is cashflow timing, internal budget signoff limits, or downside risk worrying you?\"`).",
                      "type": "paragraph"
                    },
                    {
                      "term": "Interests vs. Positions",
                      "text": "A Position is a rigid surface demand (\"Price must drop $2k\"); Interests are the genuine underlying motives, budget timing constraints, career risks, or KPIs driving why that demand feels urgent.",
                      "type": "definition"
                    },
                    {
                      "body": "\"We can’t drop our $12k project fee without trimming deliverables—help me understand, is total annual investment tight, OR strictly hitting a $6k Q2 board cap?\" -> Turns out Q3 unlocks cleanly! Split $6k June + $6k July closes full $12k cleanly!",
                      "type": "example",
                      "title": "Uncovering Cash-Timing Interest"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "steps",
                      "items": [
                        "Ask curious calm `\"What makes that metric/date key for your team?\"` questions rather than defensive `\"No way!\"` blocks.",
                        "Listen closely for **asymmetric value items**: things **cheap for you to give yet thrilling for them** (`net-60 vs upfront split, case-study shoutout, extended support window`).",
                        "Never concede ANY item free—ALWAYS attach an explicit polite `\"If we adjust X, can you confirm Y?\"` trade!"
                      ],
                      "title": "Applying Positions vs. Underlying Interests (The Orange Story)"
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "A vendor knocked 15% straight off pricing purely trying to seem cooperative—then got hit for 4 MORE discounts because giving freely signaled fat fake markup!",
                      "type": "case_study",
                      "title": "The Free Concession Trap",
                      "takeaway": "Every single adjust requires reciprocal movement (`Give-Get discipline`)!"
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Use crisp `\"If I can handle faster delivery / milestone split for you, would your team commit to upfront annual signoff / case study?\"` phrasing!",
                      "type": "callout",
                      "title": "Always Trade, Never Cave",
                      "variant": "tip"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "knowledge_check",
                      "prompt": "Why can shifting talks from rigid single-price haggling onto **Multi-Variable Trades** create simultaneous wins for both parties?",
                      "options": [
                        "Confuses legal counsel",
                        "Variables like payment speed, milestone scope, multi-year lockups, or referral rights frequently carry radically different value costs to buyer vs. seller",
                        "Delays project start"
                      ],
                      "explanation": "Asymmetric valuation across non-price terms lets both sides gain true priorities simultaneously.",
                      "correctIndex": 1
                    }
                  ],
                  "videos": [],
                  "summary": "Probing underlying interests smoothly converts stubborn tug-of-wars into inventive value-expanding deals.",
                  "position": 1,
                  "resources": [
                    {
                      "id": "bf170cc8-0505-4554-86fa-bb67de98c398",
                      "url": "https://www.pmi.org/",
                      "title": "Project Management Institute Standards & Guides",
