                      "text": "Immediacy Through Direct Perception",
                      "type": "heading",
                      "level": 2
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "In deep POV, readers already stand behind the character’s eyes; saying \"Marcus saw rain streak the glass\" doubles distance. Simply writing \"Rain streaked the glass\" puts readers right inside Marcus’s gaze cleanly.",
                      "type": "paragraph"
                    },
                    {
                      "term": "Filter Words",
                      "text": "Perception verbs (\"saw\", \"heard\", \"felt\", \"noticed\", \"wondered\") creating unnecessary narrative distance in close POV prose.",
                      "type": "definition"
                    },
                    {
                      "body": "Distanced: \"Elena heard floorboards creak behind her.\" Immediate: \"Floorboards creaked behind Elena.\"",
                      "type": "example",
                      "title": "Cutting Filter Distance"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "steps",
                      "items": [
                        "Search drafts for \"saw\", \"heard\", \"felt\", \"noticed\", \"realized\".",
                        "Rewrite straightforward perception directly as the sensation itself.",
                        "Keep deliberate perception verbs only when the act of noticing feels pivotal."
                      ],
                      "title": "Applying Removing Filter Words & Distance"
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Repeated phrases like \"she watched the headlights sweep\" slowed suspense down noticeably.",
                      "type": "case_study",
                      "title": "Muted Thriller Scene",
                      "takeaway": "\"Headlights swept the gravel\" immediately sharpened peril."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Removing filter framing drops prose straight into sensory immediacy.",
                      "type": "callout",
                      "title": "Trust Reader Immersion",
                      "variant": "tip"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "knowledge_check",
                      "prompt": "Which rewrite eliminates filter-word distance cleanly?",
                      "options": [
                        "He noticed that smoke drifted under the doorway.",
                        "Smoke drifted under the doorway.",
                        "It seemed to him he could smell smoke."
                      ],
                      "explanation": "Direct sensory rendering removes the narrator’s reporting lens cleanly.",
                      "correctIndex": 1
