                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Use `GROUP BY` and `CASE WHEN` / `FILTER` clauses to compare cohorts over consistent time buckets (ISO weeks, 30-day activation windows). Comparing apples-to-apples cohorts prevents seasonal spikes from looking like product improvements.",
                      "type": "paragraph"
                    },
                    {
                      "term": "Cohort Analysis",
                      "text": "Grouping users by shared starting period or attribute (e.g., signup week or course difficulty) to compare retention and progression fairly over time.",
                      "type": "definition"
                    },
                    {
                      "body": "Grouping `completed_at` into fixed 9-week UTC buckets (`date_trunc(\"week\", ...)`) reveals sustained study habits cleanly.",
                      "type": "example",
                      "title": "Bucketed Time Series"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "steps",
                      "items": [
                        "Break overall averages down by user segment or course level.",
                        "Keep week boundaries consistent (e.g., UTC ISO Monday start).",
                        "Compare same-age cohorts (Week-1 retention) rather than raw cumulative counts."
                      ],
                      "title": "Applying Segmentation, Cohorts & Time-Series Granularity"
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Blended completion looked steady at 64%, but segmenting by viewport exposed desktop at 78% vs mobile at 31%.",
                      "type": "case_study",
                      "title": "Hidden Mobile Drop-Off",
                      "takeaway": "Segmentation finds problems averages bury."
