                  "title": "Cross-Checking Numbers & Quantitative Claims",
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "text": "Basic Arithmetic & Logical Sanity Scans",
                      "type": "heading",
                      "level": 2
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Never glide past math claims assuming the analyst checked them twice: recompute totals, percentage deltas (`(New - Old) / Old`), per-capita scales, and prose-to-chart agreement yourself. Flag improbable claims (\"120% of surveyed users...\") or mismatched units (`$4M` vs `$4B`) loudly back to authors immediately.",
                      "type": "paragraph"
                    },
                    {
                      "term": "Relative vs. Percentage-Point Error",
                      "text": "Confusing a 50% relative jump (`20% -> 30%`) with a 50 percentage-point increase (`20% -> 70%`)—a massive quantitative misstatement in analytical prose.",
                      "type": "definition"
                    },
                    {
                      "body": "Metric rose from 10% to 15%: That is a **5 percentage-point rise** OR a **50% relative increase**—never blur the two blindly!",
                      "type": "example",
                      "title": "Fixing Percentage Framing"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "steps",
                      "items": [
                        "Verify row/column totals actually equal stated sums.",
                        "Check percentage changes explicitly (`(New - Old) / Old`).",
                        "Confirm prose claims match companion charts, tables, and caption notes 100%."
                      ],
                      "title": "Applying Cross-Checking Numbers & Quantitative Claims"
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Transposing `\"$1.4B\"` in text for `\"$1.4M\"` inside an investor deck caused awkward immediate call interruptions.",
                      "type": "case_study",
                      "title": "Billion vs. Million Slip",
                      "takeaway": "Running a dedicated isolated scan touching strictly numbers catches scale slips immediately."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Do one rapid mini-pass scanning ONLY digits, `$`, `%`, and dates—isolating quantitative context sharply focuses error detection.",
                      "type": "callout",
                      "title": "Read Numbers Isolated From Surrounding Prose",
                      "variant": "tip"
                    },
