                },
                {
                  "id": "84274380-6c74-22e2-39ef-458f52cb7b36",
                  "title": "Closing the Understanding Loop",
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "text": "Shared Meaning & Teach-Back Confirmation",
                      "type": "heading",
                      "level": 2
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Two people can nod at the word \"soon\" while picturing completely different dates: tomorrow afternoon versus next quarter. Closing the loop means turning subjective words into observable criteria and inviting listeners to summarize key constraints in their own words. When done respectfully, recap checks catch silent misunderstandings while fixes are cheap.",
                      "type": "paragraph"
                    },
                    {
                      "term": "Teach-Back Verification",
                      "text": "Asking the listener to paraphrase agreed owners, standards, and timelines without sounding condescending.",
                      "type": "definition"
                    },
                    {
                      "body": "Replace \"Send a quick summary soon\" with \"Post a 5-bullet summary in the project channel before 3 PM Friday covering blockers and budget delta.\"",
                      "type": "example",
                      "title": "Replacing Subjective Terms"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "steps",
                      "items": [
                        "Spot vague qualifiers like soon, quality, or handle it.",
                        "Convert qualifiers into numbers, timestamps, and observable deliverables.",
                        "Close spoken agreements with a written recap message naming owner and due date."
