                      "text": "Purposeful Agenda & Decision Capture",
                      "type": "heading",
                      "level": 2
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Every meeting exists either to decide, co-create, or resolve delicate alignment—rarely to read slides aloud. Sharing a crisp 3-line agenda and pre-read 24 hours earlier frees live time for genuine debate. Closing five minutes early to read back Decisions, Owners, and Dates turns talk into tracked momentum.",
                      "type": "paragraph"
                    },
                    {
                      "term": "DOD Log (Decisions, Owners, Dates)",
                      "text": "Compact closing record confirming every choice made, who holds follow-through, and the exact milestone date.",
                      "type": "definition"
                    },
                    {
                      "body": "Minutes 0–5: confirm shared problem. Minutes 5–18: weigh Option A vs B risks. Minutes 18–25: lock decision, assign owners, confirm date.",
                      "type": "example",
                      "title": "Structured 25-Minute Decision Meeting"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "steps",
                      "items": [
                        "Require an explicit objective on every invite.",
                        "Send context links early so reading happens before the call.",
                        "Capture DOD outcomes live before hanging up."
                      ],
                      "title": "Applying Facilitating Decisions & Meetings"
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "A weekly sync debated the same packaging question three weeks straight because no owner documented the resolution.",
                      "type": "case_study",
                      "title": "Recurring Circular Meetings",
                      "takeaway": "Instituting a pinned DOD log eliminated repeat debates permanently."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Protect five minutes to recap owners and give colleagues a break between calls.",
                      "type": "callout",
                      "title": "Stop at Minute 25 or 50",
                      "variant": "tip"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "knowledge_check",
                      "prompt": "Which closing step prevents recurring circular meetings?",
                      "options": [
                        "Extending the call by 20 minutes",
                        "Reading back and posting a Decisions, Owners, and Dates log before closing",
                        "Scheduling another brainstorming session immediately"
                      ],
                      "explanation": "Explicit written ownership locks in resolution and eliminates repeat debate.",
                      "correctIndex": 1
