            {
              "id": "32972420-0124-bd23-5d67-5630c1466bf3",
              "title": "Module 2: Margin Mechanics, Markup Myths & Break-Even",
              "lessons": [
                {
                  "id": "6e87b57b-5d58-8f3e-0493-29a5c8f5b5cc",
                  "title": "The Costly Markup vs. Gross Margin Blunder",
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "text": "Gross Margin vs. Cost Markup Formulas",
                      "type": "heading",
                      "level": 2
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "**Markup %** adds onto Cost (`(Price - Cost) / Cost`), whereas **Gross Margin %** measures profit retained out of total Selling Price (`(Price - Cost) / Price`). Confusing both is catastrophic when offering \"25% off holiday promos!\" on items carrying only 28% markup! To price cleanly for a target **Target Margin (`M`)**, always divide strictly: `Price = Cost / (1 - Target Margin %)`.",
                      "type": "paragraph"
                    },
                    {
                      "term": "Margin Pricing Formula",
                      "text": "Computing exact selling price to hit a desired Gross Margin percentage: `Selling Price = Unit Cost / (1 - Target Margin Decimal)` (e.g., `$60 / (1 - 0.40) = $100`).",
                      "type": "definition"
                    },
                    {
                      "body": "Cost: $60. Wrong (+40% Markup): `$60 x 1.4 = $84` -> Profit `$24 / $84 = 28.6%` Margin (Shortfall!). Correct (`40% Margin`): `$60 / (1 - 0.40) = $100` -> Profit `$40 / $100 = 40.0%` Margin cleanly!",
                      "type": "example",
                      "title": "Fixing $60 Product Math"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "steps",
                      "items": [
                        "NEVER add Target Margin % directly onto Cost!",
                        "Use `Price = Cost / (1 - Desired Margin)` consistently in every quote spreadsheet.",
                        "Before offering \"20% Holiday Discount!\", compute post-discount Gross Margin—never accidentally sell below variable fulfillment costs!"
                      ],
                      "title": "Applying The Costly Markup vs. Gross Margin Blunder"
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "A boutique retail brand ran \"30% Off Sitewide!\" assuming their 40% \"markup\" left plenty of profit—40% markup equals only 28.6% margin, turning every discounted order literally negative net after shipping!",
