            {
              "id": "c1871262-7ca7-a886-6206-003903f76acc",
              "title": "Module 3: Jobs-to-be-Done (JTBD) & Switching Triggers",
              "lessons": [
                {
                  "id": "858d468a-f761-3b98-1035-525c4b40dc7d",
                  "title": "Customers \"Hire\" Products to Get a Job Done",
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "text": "Functional, Emotional & Social Jobs",
                      "type": "heading",
                      "level": 2
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Clayton Christensen’s **Jobs-to-be-Done (`JTBD`)** lens exposes true hidden competition: a fast-food milkshake morning commuter isn’t competing strictly against other ice cream shops—it competes directly against bagels, bananas, and podcasts for the job of `\"Keeping my commute calm, clean-handed, and full until noon!\"`. Seeing true job competition transforms positioning overnight.",
                      "type": "paragraph"
                    },
                    {
                      "term": "Jobs-to-be-Done (JTBD)",
                      "text": "Research framework examining the underlying practical, emotional, and social progress a buyer seeks when \"hiring\" a solution into a specific life or work context.",
                      "type": "definition"
                    },
                    {
                      "body": "A clean lightweight invoice template tool realized its fiercest real rival wasn’t heavy accounting enterprise suites—it was `\"Copying old Word docs Sunday night\"` because users prized zero-setup speed!",
