                      "type": "heading",
                      "level": 2
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Distance kills audio: move microphones within 6–12 inches of the speaker (or record inside carpeted furnished rooms absorbing echo), normalize spoken dialogue consistently around `-14 to -16 LUFS` (`-6 dB` peaks), sit subtle ambient underscore music low beneath dialogue (`-24 to -30 dB` ducked cleanly—never competing!), and add subtle 3-frame audio crossfades preventing clicks across cuts.",
                      "type": "paragraph"
                    },
                    {
                      "term": "Audio Crossfade / Ducking",
                      "text": "Short subtle volume dissolves smoothing sudden audio cut pops alongside automatically lowering background music cleanly underneath spoken words.",
                      "type": "definition"
                    },
                    {
                      "body": "Voice peaks clearly around `-6 dB` while gentle background track sits subtly near `-26 dB`—audible as quiet energy, never fighting intelligibility.",
                      "type": "example",
                      "title": "Clean Music Balance"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "steps",
                      "items": [
                        "Record speech closer to soft furnishings (curtains, rugs) rather than bare tiled hallways.",
