                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Real physical motion accelerates and decelerates smoothly (**Ease Out** for snappy quick entries settling gently into rest; **Ease In** accelerating cleanly out of frame). Keep functional UI callouts and lower-thirds tight (`0.25s–0.45s` transitions!), avoid dizzy spinning transitions everywhere, and never add motion strictly to mask weak composition.",
                      "type": "paragraph"
                    },
                    {
                      "term": "Easing Curve (Ease Out / Cubic Bezier)",
                      "text": "Adjusting animation velocity curves so moving titles arrive crisply and settle gently with natural momentum rather than stiff constant robot speed.",
                      "type": "definition"
                    },
                    {
                      "body": "0.0s–0.35s: Smooth Ease-Out slide/fade up from baseline mask -> 0.35s–3.5s: Rock-steady readable hold -> 3.5s–3.8s: Clean subtle fade out.",
                      "type": "example",
                      "title": "Crisp Lower-Third Timing"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "steps",
                      "items": [
                        "Replace default linear keyframes everywhere with custom Ease-Out arrivals.",
                        "Keep transition duration disciplined (`~0.3s` for titles/cards; `>0.8s` feels sluggish!).",
                        "Stagger linked elements slightly (`40–60ms`) rather than exploding all layers at millisecond zero."
                      ],
                      "title": "Applying Natural Physics: Easing, Anticipation & Restraint"
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Trimming a 6-second spinning neon logo intro down to a snappy 0.9-second eased corner badge stopped 25% of viewers clicking away before the speaker even started talking.",
                      "type": "case_study",
