                  "title": "Correction First, Creative Grading Second",
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "text": "Neutralizing Clips Before Styling Mood",
                      "type": "heading",
                      "level": 2
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Always divide color work cleanly into two separate steps: **1. Color Correction**—match brightness/exposure, set neutral blacks/whites, fix greenish/orange White Balance cast across every timeline clip so Clip A and Clip B look filmed in the same room cleanly; **2. Color Grading**—apply subtle unified contrast curves or gentle mood warmth gently across the matched sequence.",
                      "type": "paragraph"
                    },
                    {
                      "term": "Color Correction vs. Grading",
                      "text": "Correction fixes technical exposure and true neutral white balance across clips cleanly; Grading applies intentional artistic mood/palette style afterward.",
                      "type": "definition"
                    },
                    {
                      "body": "Main A-cam shot near window (cool 5600K) + B-cam under tungsten desk lamp (warm 3200K) -> Warm A-cam slightly and cool B-cam slightly until crisp white shirts match neutral cleanly on both.",
                      "type": "example",
                      "title": "Matching Multi-Camera Clips"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "steps",
                      "items": [
                        "Use Waveform/Parade scopes rather than trusting bright uncalibrated laptop screens blindly.",
                        "Set neutral grey/white reference points first before touching saturation.",
                        "Keep saturation restrained—oversaturated neon skin tones scream amateur fast."
                      ],
                      "title": "Applying Correction First, Creative Grading Second"
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Matching exposure and white balance across two different phone models took 15 minutes and made an indoor-to-outdoor mini-doc feel completely cohesive.",
                      "type": "case_study",
                      "title": "Seamless Dual-Phone Documentary",
                      "takeaway": "Technical matching quietly eliminates jarring viewer distraction."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Human skin tones across all ethnicities fall remarkably tightly along one diagonal line on video Vectorscopes—verify faces sit naturally there!",
                      "type": "callout",
                      "title": "Check Skin Tone Vectorscope Line",
                      "variant": "tip"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "knowledge_check",
                      "prompt": "Why must Color Correction always precede creative LUT or Color Grading filters?",
                      "options": [
                        "LUTs disable timelines",
                        "Creative filters multiply existing exposure and white-balance errors, locking weird color casts across uneven clips",
                        "Correction deletes audio"
                      ],
                      "explanation": "Normalizing clips to honest clean exposure lets subtle mood grades shine cleanly.",
                      "correctIndex": 1
