                      "text": "Right-Sizing Pixels & Web Formats",
                      "type": "heading",
                      "level": 2
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Resize images cleanly to realistic maximum display dimensions (`1600–2000px` wide for full web heroes; `800–1200px` for article cards—never 6000px raw camera widths!), export photos as modern `WebP` (`78–84%` quality) or crisp `JPEG` keeping web files comfortably `< 250 KB`, reserve `PNG`/`SVG` strictly for transparent graphics/logos, and save crisp `300 DPI` originals solely for physical print.",
                      "type": "paragraph"
                    },
                    {
                      "term": "Display Pixel Sizing",
                      "text": "Scaling image pixel dimensions down to actual display requirements (`~1600px` hero / `~1080px` social) before compression—often slashing file weight by 85% cleanly.",
                      "type": "definition"
                    },
                    {
                      "body": "Compressing six 6MB raw hero photos down cleanly to `1600px` WebP (`140 KB` each) dropped page load time from 6.8s down to 1.2s with identical crisp screen beauty.",
                      "type": "example",
                      "title": "Web Speed Rescue"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "steps",
                      "items": [
                        "Keep untouched master RAW/high-res masters backed up safely before creating resized web/social copies.",
                        "Use `sRGB` color profile strictly for ALL web and social image exports (AdobeRGB washes pale dull in browsers!).",
                        "Verify `alt` text and crisp thumbnails after upload."
                      ],
                      "title": "Applying Image Compression Without Muddy Artifacts"
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Converting client gallery exports cleanly from camera `AdobeRGB` profile to `sRGB` immediately restored vibrant accurate reds and deep skin tones across Safari and Chrome.",
                      "type": "case_study",
                      "title": "Washed-Out Browser Photo Fix",
                      "takeaway": "Always export web/social images in `sRGB` color space."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Browsers assume sRGB cleanly; wide-gamut print profiles desaturate visibly on standard phones and laptops.",
                      "type": "callout",
                      "title": "Always Convert Web Photos to sRGB",
                      "variant": "tip"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "knowledge_check",
                      "prompt": "Why must photographers export web and social media JPEGs/WebPs strictly in `sRGB` color profile?",
                      "options": [
                        "sRGB doubles pixel count",
                        "Web browsers and social apps expect sRGB; wider profiles like AdobeRGB often render muddy and washed out online",
