                {
                  "id": "13cfac1b-5f1b-7f4c-af76-402fbe154154",
                  "title": "Protecting Capacity for Quality & Tech Debt",
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "text": "Sustaining Velocity via Clean Upkeep",
                      "type": "heading",
                      "level": 2
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "If product managers squeeze 100% of every sprint strictly into outward user-facing shiny tabs while dismissing performance latency, brittle test gaps, and messy database migrations, velocity grinds painfully toward zero (`compounding technical debt`). Partner closely with engineering leads locking predictable ongoing upkeep (**~15–20% continuous capacity** OR clear customer-impact budgets) keeping release velocity nimble permanently!",
                      "type": "paragraph"
                    },
                    {
                      "term": "Compounding Technical Debt",
                      "text": "Cumulative engineering shortcuts and deferred maintenance steadily slowing every future feature build, increasing production bugs, and raising onboarding lag for new developers.",
                      "type": "definition"
                    },
                    {
                      "body": "Never pitch `\"Refactor legacy SQL layer\"` blindly—frame impact clearly: `\"Cleaning invoice indexing shrinks page timeouts for heavy accounts from 9s down to 0.4s AND cuts checkout sprint risk 50%.\"`",
                      "type": "example",
                      "title": "Translating Tech Debt into User Language"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "steps",
                      "items": [
                        "Co-own platform speed, reliability (`uptime, latency`), and clean error rates right alongside engineering leads.",
                        "Reserve steady explicit 15–20% continuous maintenance capacity cleanly rather than begging twice a year for desperate \"6-week Code Freeze\" stops!",
                        "Fix high-traffic fragile hotspots iteratively right when modifying adjacent flows (`Boy Scout rule`)."
                      ],
                      "title": "Applying Protecting Capacity for Quality & Tech Debt"
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Eighteen months of pure blind feature sprints left code so brittle even changing a minor pricing button crashed billing webhooks—forcing a painful 3-month total product stall.",
                      "type": "case_study",
                      "title": "The Six-Month Feature Freeze",
                      "takeaway": "Steady modest ongoing platform care safeguards continuous smooth shipping!"
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Help leadership see plainly how faster reliable tools protect retention and future build speed directly!",
                      "type": "callout",
                      "title": "Connect Cleanup Straight to Customer Impact",
                      "variant": "tip"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "knowledge_check",
                      "prompt": "Why lock a continuous ~15–20% engineering upkeep allocation inside healthy product roadmaps instead of skipping maintenance until systems break?",
                      "options": [
                        "Developers prefer slow coding",
                        "Prevents compounding fragility that silently slows future features from days into agonizing error-prone weeks",
                        "Reduces cloud logs"
                      ],
                      "explanation": "Ongoing steady upkeep preserves agile shipping cadence while protecting site speed and reliability seamlessly.",
                      "correctIndex": 1
