                    {
                      "text": "Honest Uncertainty Framing",
                      "type": "heading",
                      "level": 2
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Strong professionals state what is verified, what remains in testing, and what contingency triggers apply—without sounding panicked or evasive. Replacing absolute guarantees with concrete conditional triggers shows mature judgment.",
                      "type": "paragraph"
                    },
                    {
                      "term": "Risk Contingency Statement",
                      "text": "An explicit pairing of a known delivery risk with its preventative action or fallback trigger date.",
                      "type": "definition"
                    },
                    {
                      "body": "\"Current QA confirms desktop readiness (88% coverage). Mobile payment testing finishes Thursday 3 PM; if critical bugs persist, we will release desktop Friday and ship mobile Monday.\"",
                      "type": "example",
                      "title": "Framing Uncertainty Cleanly"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "steps",
                      "items": [
                        "Separate verified facts from open assumptions explicitly.",
                        "Attach realistic fallback dates to every external dependency.",
                        "Own team next steps clearly without blaming partner bottlenecks."
                      ],
                      "title": "Applying Managing Risk, Caveats & Accountability"
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Telling a client \"everything will launch smoothly Monday\" without mentioning unverified API keys backfired when third-party onboarding stalled.",
                      "type": "case_study",
                      "title": "Unqualified Launch Promise",
                      "takeaway": "Pre-labeling dependencies prevented last-minute surprises cleanly."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Never surface an open problem in writing without attaching your concrete mitigation or fallback option.",
