                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "text": "The No-Surprises Ownership Rule",
                      "type": "heading",
                      "level": 2
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Never miss a commitment quietly. The instant scope turns out larger or a dependency stalls, notify stakeholders early with: 1) What changed, 2) Current realistic ETA, and 3) Proposed trade-off (\"Option A: deliver core flow on time and polish animations Friday; Option B: shift launch 1 day\"). Teams trust people who surface surprises early.",
                      "type": "paragraph"
                    },
                    {
                      "term": "Proactive Risk Escalation",
                      "text": "Communicating schedule or scope risks early alongside concrete trade-off options while there is still time to adjust cleanly.",
                      "type": "definition"
                    },
                    {
                      "body": "\"Heads up: API pagination edge case took longer than scoped. Core table is ready today; full CSV export will land Wed 11am unless we want to ship export separately.\"",
                      "type": "example",
                      "title": "Calm Delay Update"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "steps",
                      "items": [
                        "Confirm exact deadlines and timezones when accepting tasks.",
                        "Surface blockers the same day they resist 30–60 minutes of solo troubleshooting.",
                        "Deliver what you promised—or renegotiate early, never late."
                      ],
                      "title": "Applying Owning Commitments & Raising Early Alerts"
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Flagging a vendor delay 48 hours ahead allowed leadership to swap launch copy smoothly with zero customer impact.",
                      "type": "case_study",
                      "title": "Earned Lead Trust Under Pressure",
                      "takeaway": "Reliability shines brightest when plans shift."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "When you drop a ball, state it plainly, fix it cleanly, and adjust your checklist—skip long defensive stories.",
