                {
                  "id": "91d140df-90d5-3c1f-9771-2f11761da91f",
                  "title": "Trade-Offs: Defining Strategy by What You Say NO To",
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "text": "Intentional Asymmetric Trade-Offs",
                      "type": "heading",
                      "level": 2
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Michael Porter taught the timeless rule: **the essence of strategy is choosing what NOT to do!** Anyone copies good best practices (`clean site, quick replies`); real durable positioning chooses incompatible advantages purposefully—like Southwest cutting reserved seats & meals to deliver unmatched fast turnaround low fares, while boutique luxury airlines embrace high overhead intentionally for high-fare comfort.",
                      "type": "paragraph"
                    },
                    {
                      "term": "Strategic Trade-Off",
                      "text": "Intentionally sacrificing incompatible segments, features, or service styles so internal operations, pricing, and brand promise optimize cleanly for one unmistakable core audience.",
                      "type": "definition"
                    },
                    {
                      "body": "Fast-casual café deliberately refuses table reservations, 60-item menus, and complex off-menu modifications—freeing kitchen speed and ingredient freshness to serve hot gourmet lunches cleanly inside 4 minutes at $12!",
                      "type": "example",
                      "title": "Clear Strategic Refusal"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "steps",
                      "items": [
                        "List 3 attractive customer types or features your brand deliberately **walks away from** (`\"Straddling trap avoidance\"`).",
                        "Check internal processes: do budgets, hiring, and incentives genuinely support your side of the trade-off?",
                        "Revisit discarded opportunities peacefully once a year rather than weekly distraction whipsaws!"
                      ],
                      "title": "Applying Trade-Offs: Defining Strategy by What You Say NO To"
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "A boutique design agency added \"$99 template tweaks\" trying to capture bargain hunters while pitching $40k brand transformations—diluting prestige for whales AND losing money on cheap rush jobs!",
                      "type": "case_study",
                      "title": "The Miserable Middle Straddle",
                      "takeaway": "Dropping cheap rush tiers immediately rebuilt premium margins cleanly!"
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Genuine strategic choices carry crisp visible opportunity costs—if zero stakeholders feel a pinch letting something go, nothing real got prioritized!",
                      "type": "callout",
                      "title": "If Everyone Agrees, It’s Probably Not Strategy",
                      "variant": "tip"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "knowledge_check",
                      "prompt": "Why did Southwest Airlines’ original refusal of hot meals, seat assignments, and airport lounges serve as genuine strategic strength rather than a lazy flaw?",
                      "options": [
