                {
                  "id": "9b239a45-21ac-722d-45f5-b46928da05c4",
                  "title": "First-Order vs. Second-Order Thinking",
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "text": "Thinking Ten Moves Down the Chessboard",
                      "type": "heading",
                      "level": 2
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "**First-order thinkers** optimize strictly immediate obvious payoff (`\"Do X -> Get quick win Y\"`). **Second-order thinkers** ask relentlessly: **\"And then what happens next... and after THAT?\"** Notice how aggressive \"30% Off Month-End Sales!\" trains loyal buyers purely to stall purchases cleanly until every final 48-hour discount window!",
                      "type": "paragraph"
                    },
                    {
                      "term": "Second-Order Thinking",
                      "text": "Evaluating not only the immediate direct payoff of a decision (`1st order`), but the ripple incentives, customer behavior shifts, and competitive responses unfolding 3–12 months later (`2nd/3rd order`).",
                      "type": "definition"
                    },
                    {
                      "body": "1st Order: Paying support reps strictly per closed ticket boosts daily ticket count +40%! 2nd Order: Reps start rushing superficial copy-paste replies forcing furious customers to reopen 3 follow-up tickets (`destroying trust while secretly tripling volume!`).",
                      "type": "example",
                      "title": "The Per-Ticket Support Quota Trap"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "steps",
                      "items": [
                        "Pause before signing big policy incentives asking aloud: **\"How will smart rational humans game this exact rule?\"** (`Goodhart’s Law`)",
                        "Trace impacts cleanly at **Day 7**, **Month 3**, and **Year 1** before locking irreversible shifts.",
                        "Pair volume metrics always alongside opposing quality counter-metrics (`speed + CSAT + non-reopen rate`)!"
                      ],
                      "title": "Applying First-Order vs. Second-Order Thinking"
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Bounties paid per captured pest caused opportunistic breeders to raise pests purely for government payout—multiplying the exact problem once canceled!",
                      "type": "case_study",
                      "title": "The Cobra Effect in Action",
                      "takeaway": "Always ask how incentives reshape real human behavior subtly over months!"
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Any single isolated KPI pushed ruthlessly (`clicks, closed tickets, short calls`) bends human behavior cleverly away from true customer value!",
                      "type": "callout",
                      "title": "Watch Metric Gaming Early",
                      "variant": "tip"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "knowledge_check",
                      "prompt": "When evaluating a proposed \"50% Off Flash Sale Every Final Weekend\" tactic, what crucial 2nd-order consequence hurts long-term business health?",
                      "options": [
                        "Email providers complain",
                        "Loyal full-price customers quickly learn to wait patiently until month-end discounts, crushing steady full-margin demand cleanly",
                        "Inventory stays cleaner"
                      ],
                      "explanation": "Anticipating shifting buyer habits beyond immediate weekend spikes prevents training price dependency.",
                      "correctIndex": 1
                    }
