                  "id": "547d966e-91f1-c1ef-6e82-a4b151fc1ded",
                  "title": "One-Way vs. Two-Way Doors (Decision Speed)",
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "text": "Match Analysis Depth Strictly to Reversibility",
                      "type": "heading",
                      "level": 2
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Jeff Bezos popularized separating **Type 1 (`One-Way Doors`: nearly irreversible, high stakes -> slow deep scrutiny + broad challenge!)** sharply from **Type 2 (`Two-Way Doors`: easily tested and reverted within days cleanly -> decide fast cleanly with small empowered leads!)**. Applying heavy slow Type-1 bureaucracy onto minor Type-2 choices kills momentum completely.",
                      "type": "paragraph"
                    },
                    {
                      "term": "Two-Way Door Decision",
                      "text": "A reversible choice easily tested or undone cheaply within days/weeks if results disappoint—should be delegated cleanly and shipped fast without slow committee consensus.",
                      "type": "definition"
                    },
                    {
                      "body": "Two-Way Door: Testing a 14-day onboarding email tweak -> Ship fast Tuesday! One-Way Door: Abandoning core legacy brand name OR signing a 5-year warehouse lease -> Deep financial stress-testing + Pre-Mortem required!",
                      "type": "example",
                      "title": "Correct Door Calibration"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "steps",
                      "items": [
                        "Label major weekly agenda items explicitly as `[2-Way Test]` vs. `[1-Way Commitment]` right in meeting titles.",
                        "Empower frontline owners cleanly over Two-Way tests with explicit cheap Stop-Loss guardrails.",
                        "Slow down deliberately strictly on irreversible One-Way traps!"
                      ],
                      "title": "Applying One-Way vs. Two-Way Doors (Decision Speed)"
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "A 25-person team debated a completely reversible $300 homepage copy test across four full 1-hour executive meetings—wasting 20x the cost of simply running the live test cleanly for 7 days!",
                      "type": "case_study",
                      "title": "Committee Paralysis Slowdown",
                      "takeaway": "Sorting reversible tests cleanly into fast 7-day loops sped shipping 4x!"
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Whenever real market behavior stays uncertain, run an honest small real-world check instead of guessing over 30 speculative spreadsheet columns!",
