                      "text": "Evaluating Inductive Strength",
                      "type": "heading",
                      "level": 2
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Deductive rules guarantee conclusions if valid (\"All EU orders need VAT -> this is an EU order\"); inductive predictions generalize from limited observations (\"Five pilot clinics loved Feature X -> all 400 clinics will love it\"). Measuring sample size, representativeness, and counter-examples keeps inductive claims honest and proportionate.",
                      "type": "paragraph"
                    },
                    {
                      "term": "Sample Representativeness",
                      "text": "Whether observed pilot cases genuinely share constraints, budget, and habits of the broader target population.",
                      "type": "definition"
                    },
                    {
                      "body": "If all five happy pilot users were tech startups with dedicated IT, generalizing directly to small rural clinics breaks cleanly.",
                      "type": "example",
                      "title": "Checking Pilot Skew"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "steps",
                      "items": [
                        "Verify whether claims sound absolute (\"proves!\") despite probabilistic samples.",
                        "Check who was excluded from sample observations.",
                        "Adjust confidence claims proportionally to sample diversity."
                      ],
                      "title": "Applying Deductive Certainty vs. Inductive Evidence"
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Surveying only top-10 power users yielded 95% praise for complex shortcuts that overwhelmed actual new buyers.",
                      "type": "case_study",
                      "title": "Biased Early Survey Trap",
                      "takeaway": "Representative samples beat skewed enthusiasm every time."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Always inspect non-responders and quiet dropouts alongside loud enthusiastic feedback.",
                      "type": "callout",
                      "title": "Ask \"Who Was Missing From the Room?\"",
                      "variant": "tip"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "knowledge_check",
                      "prompt": "Why should generalizing broadly from 5 enthusiastic pilot clients be scrutinized carefully?",
                      "options": [
                        "Small samples prove universal certainty",
                        "Early volunteers often differ systematically in tech skill or enthusiasm from broader future buyers",
                        "Quantitative data cannot apply to UX"
                      ],
                      "explanation": "Checking sample bias avoids building for unrepresentative outliers.",
                      "correctIndex": 1
