                },
                {
                  "id": "5e8eb87c-a41f-a22b-5ef0-8ea9dbdb3b4c",
                  "title": "Correlation, Causation & Number Tricks",
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "text": "Lurking Variables & Denominator Traps",
                      "type": "heading",
                      "level": 2
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Ice-cream sales and drowning incidents rise together every July—both driven by summer heat (confounding variable), not ice cream causing harm. Similarly, misleading reports tout \"+100% growth!\" when metrics climb from 1 user to 2, or truncate Y-axis scales to exaggerate tiny 0.4% shifts. Always inspect sample denominators, confounders, and full baseline scale.",
                      "type": "paragraph"
                    },
                    {
                      "term": "Confounding Variable",
                      "text": "An unseen third factor driving simultaneous movement in two seemingly connected variables, creating false causal illusion.",
                      "type": "definition"
                    },
                    {
                      "body": "Users taking dark-mode tour spend 3x more -> not caused by dark mode; high-intent power buyers explore settings way more naturally.",
                      "type": "example",
                      "title": "App Engagement Illusion"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "steps",
                      "items": [
                        "Ask: \"Could C drive both A and B? Or could B cause A?\"",
                        "Check raw denominators behind dramatic percentages.",
                        "Inspect baseline zero axes on visual comparison charts."
                      ],
                      "title": "Applying Correlation, Causation & Number Tricks"
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Controlled A/B tests proved onboarding badges correlated with engaged users rather than causing retention—preventing six weeks of wasted badge redesigns.",
                      "type": "case_study",
                      "title": "Avoiding Wrong Product Investments",
                      "takeaway": "Causal verification guards scarce engineering time cleanly."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "\"Double conversions!\" means nothing until knowing whether sample equaled 2 vs 4 or 2,000 vs 4,000.",
                      "type": "callout",
                      "title": "Always Ask \"Out of How Many?\"",
                      "variant": "tip"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "knowledge_check",
                      "prompt": "Why might customers who read Help Articles show higher retention without Help Articles actually causing the retention?",
                      "options": [
                        "Reading lowers bandwidth",
                        "Confounding variable: highly motivated power users both read docs and renew naturally",
                        "Articles break analytics cookies"
                      ],
                      "explanation": "Motivation confounds correlation cleanly unless tested with randomized control.",
                      "correctIndex": 1
                    }
                  ],
                  "videos": [],
                  "summary": "Basic causal literacy guards decisions against slick chart illusions.",
                  "position": 2,
