                      "text": "Imperative Single-Action Steps",
                      "type": "heading",
                      "level": 2
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Procedural docs rely on active second-person imperatives: \"Click **Settings** -> **Webhooks**, then select **Add Endpoint**.\" Keeping one logical user click or command per step—never cramming four nested clicks into one dense line—prevents accidental skipped steps under stress.",
                      "type": "paragraph"
                    },
                    {
                      "term": "Location Before Action",
                      "text": "Naming where to click or focus first (\"In the **Billing** sidebar, click **Update Card**\") so the eye locates the control before pressing it.",
                      "type": "definition"
                    },
                    {
                      "body": "Slow: \"Click **Save** at the bottom right of the Billing Settings panel.\" Fast: \"In **Billing Settings**, click **Save**.\"",
                      "type": "example",
                      "title": "Location-First Phrasing"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "steps",
                      "items": [
                        "Open steps with screen area first, exact bold UI label second.",
                        "Isolate code snippets cleanly inside copyable code blocks (`bash`, `json`).",
                        "Separate conditional branches cleanly (\"If upgrading from v1...\")."
                      ],
                      "title": "Applying Active Imperative Steps & Scannable Commands"
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Burying four UI transitions inside one bulleted line led 30% of admins to miss toggling webhook signing.",
                      "type": "case_study",
                      "title": "Skipped Sub-Menu Click",
                      "takeaway": "Breaking into crisp numbered sub-steps eliminated config errors completely."
                    },
