                {
                  "id": "4b8c4c26-3df5-8477-f375-91064c0279a6",
                  "title": "User Flows, Navigation & Happy/Sad Paths",
                  "blocks": [
                    {
                      "text": "Designing Complete Task Flows & Edge States",
                      "type": "heading",
                      "level": 2
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Before styling polished buttons, map every screen along a User Flow (`Entry -> Task Steps -> Success Confirmation`) AND design all four UI states cleanly: **Ideal Populated**, **Empty/First-Run** (helpful CTA!), **Loading Skeleton**, and **Error/Offline Recovery**.",
                      "type": "paragraph"
                    },
                    {
                      "term": "Four Core UI States",
                      "text": "Ideal (populated data), Empty (helpful onboarding action), Loading (calm skeleton progress), and Error (non-blaming recovery guidance).",
                      "type": "definition"
                    },
                    {
                      "body": "Never leave a blank screen reading `No items.` Instead write: \"No saved projects yet — start your first 5-minute brief\" + crisp button.",
                      "type": "example",
                      "title": "Helpful Empty State"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "steps",
                      "items": [
                        "Draw explicit entry points, decision diamonds, and back/cancel exits on flows.",
                        "Design Empty, Loading, and Error states right beside every primary list screen.",
                        "Verify navigation labels match real user vocabulary rather than internal company jargon."
                      ],
                      "title": "Applying User Flows, Navigation & Happy/Sad Paths"
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Mapping complete flows caught an orphaned password-reset confirmation screen lacking any link back to sign-in.",
                      "type": "case_study",
                      "title": "Prevented Dead-End Mobile Flow",
                      "takeaway": "User flow mapping catches navigation traps cleanly before coding starts."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Users look for \"Pricing\" and \"Orders\"—never obscure branded terms like \"Value Matrix\" in primary nav bars.",
                      "type": "callout",
