                      "text": "Safe Branch Sync & Conflict Resolution",
                      "type": "heading",
                      "level": 2
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Branch off fresh main (git checkout -b fix/nav-wrap), commit work, fetch origin main, merge or rebase locally, inspect every <<<<<<< / ======= / >>>>>>> block thoughtfully, run tests AFTER resolving conflicts, and push cleanly.",
                      "type": "paragraph"
                    },
                    {
                      "term": "Merge Conflict",
                      "text": "Occurs when two branches alter overlapping lines in the same file, requiring human choice to combine both intents accurately.",
                      "type": "definition"
                    },
                    {
                      "body": "Never commit conflict resolutions blindly—run lint and automated tests after saving resolved files to confirm syntax and logic remain intact.",
                      "type": "example",
                      "title": "Post-Merge Verification"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "steps",
                      "items": [
                        "Pull latest main before starting each new branch.",
                        "Read both sides of conflict markers to preserve both teammates’ requirements.",
                        "Delete merged feature branches to keep repository tidy."
                      ],
                      "title": "Applying Feature Branches, Rebasing & Merge Conflict Resolution"
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Resolving a conflict in repository queries kept both teammate’s sorting fix and your explicit FK disambiguation cleanly.",
                      "type": "case_study",
                      "title": "Preserved Dual Fix",
                      "takeaway": "Understanding both sides prevents silent regressions."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Reserve force-with-lease strictly for personal unmerged feature branches—never rewrite shared main history.",
                      "type": "callout",
                      "title": "Never Force-Push Shared Main",
                      "variant": "tip"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "knowledge_check",
                      "prompt": "What must ALWAYS happen right after resolving Git merge conflict markers?",
                      "options": [
