                      "text": "Self-Awareness Under Pressure",
                      "type": "heading",
                      "level": 2
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Everyone drifts under sharp tension: some steamroll loudly, others cave hastily to dodge discomfort, others retreat into cold silence. Recognizing your personal adrenaline cues (fast heartbeat, tight jaw, urge to rush replies) lets you pause three seconds, breathe quietly, and pick principled constructive engagement intentionally.",
                      "type": "paragraph"
                    },
                    {
                      "term": "Stress Reflex Profile",
                      "text": "Understanding your automatic default tendency under confrontation (competing, avoiding, accommodating, or collaborating) to widen deliberate choice.",
                      "type": "definition"
                    },
                    {
                      "body": "Notice when you rush \"Whatever you want is fine!\" to flee heat -> pause -> state \"Actually, let's inspect both constraints calmly at 2 PM.\"",
                      "type": "example",
                      "title": "Overcoming Default Appeasing"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "steps",
                      "items": [
                        "Identify your top physiological stress cue.",
                        "Pair each default reflex with one calming counter-habit.",
                        "Practice delicate low-stakes boundary conversations weekly."
                      ],
                      "title": "Applying Personal Conflict Resilience Plan"
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Recognizing an impulse to shut down silently allowed a lead architect to take a crisp breath and voice engineering safety constraints cleanly.",
                      "type": "case_study",
                      "title": "Steady Lead Growth",
                      "takeaway": "Self-command anchors every other conflict tool."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Three steady breaths restore prefrontal judgment before speaking in tense rooms.",
